  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1226 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nDrier air will spread eastward across the CWA Saturday in tandem\nwith 591 dam ridge, keeping conditions quiet and warmer than we\nhave experienced over the last several days. This quiet period \nwill be brief, as a strong trough begins to dig in from the \nPacific coast Sunday and Monday. South-southwesterly flow ahead \nof this trough will begin to draw moisture back into the region, \nwith shower and thunderstorm chances increasing once again late \nSunday and moreso on Monday.\n\nBy this point, confidence in forecast trends begins to lower due \nto spread in model solutions, particularly in regards to the \nevolution of the trough and moisture plume influence from \nHurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific. A slower progression of the\ntrough would favor a wetter scenario as the ECMWF deterministic \nand ensemble models are showcasing, whereas a faster progression \nlike what the GFS and GFS Ens show would result in a quicker \nreturn to drier conditions. For now, expect precipitation chances \nto peak sometime Monday into Tuesday before the trough traverses \neastward and a drier pattern takes hold by mid to late week. \nDetails will be refined in the coming days.",
